Optionally drop the CJK encodings from mbstring to shrink the image.

The bundled libmbfl carries the legacy CJK (Chinese/Japanese/Korean) codecs and their
tables in mbfilter_cjk.c (and mbfilter_utf8_mobile.c, which reuses its emoji tables). With
the mbstring-cjk option off, the sdk leaves those files (and nls_ja/kr/zh.c) out of the build
and defines MBSTRING_NO_CJK; this patch then drops, behind the macro, everything that would
otherwise reference the missing encodings: the CJK and mobile UTF-8 entries in the encoding
registry, the ja/ko/zh-cn/zh-tw languages (whose detect lists and mail charsets are CJK
encodings, so mb_language() rejects them with a ValueError), the sjis_mac fast-path check in
mb_substr, and the mb_convert_kana() function (Japanese kana, backed by a table in
mbfilter_cjk.c). UTF-8/UTF-16/Latin and everything else keep working. Without the macro this
is a no-op (all encodings, languages and mb_convert_kana present).
